# Norton Big Four WD military combination, 633 cc side-valve single with a
# driven sidecar wheel.
#
# The Big Four was Norton's sidecar machine: a rigid frame, girder fork, 633 cc
# side-valve single of about 14 hp, wide-ratio trials gearbox and a live axle
# driving the sidecar wheel through a dog clutch (the Baughan system, adopted by
# the War Department in 1938). About 4,500 were built for the WD, from November
# 1939 (S1000) to autumn 1941 (S5308). It carried three men: driver, pillion and
# one in the chair, with kit.
# https://www.nortonownersclub.org/history/1936-1945-wd
# https://www.militarytrader.com/military-vehicles/a-look-at-the-british-norton-big-4
#
# Dimensions: overall just over 8 ft (about 2.44 m) long, wheelbase 4 ft 6.25 in
# (1.38 m), 2 ft 6.5 in (0.78 m) over the handlebars; wheels 4.00 x 18 in. The
# sidecar body is a rectangular military tub with a rounded lower nose, high
# sides, an open top and a spare wheel on the rear. Unladen weight over 400 kg.
# https://www.militarytrader.com/military-vehicles/a-look-at-the-british-norton-big-4
#
# Colour: khaki green No. 3 / WD green, the wartime finish. One colourway only
# (a military vehicle), so the model is a single object.
# https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C968214
#
# Weight: about 4,500 built, almost all for the Army and Commonwealth forces, so
# the outfit was a rare sight on civilian roads during 1940-45; ex-WD machines
# were sold off after the war. Set at 40 on the 2026-11-15 scale against the
# 1940s anchors. Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 80 km/h (50 mph): a game-balance figure; the WD machine was geared low
# for sidecar work. Comfort 25/190: rigid frame, girder fork, sprung saddle and
# an open military tub.
#
# Dates describe when the outfit was seen on British roads: wartime service from
# 1940, then ex-WD civilian use to the late 1940s.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/norton-big-four-combo.blend in the blends
# repository. References: citycars/references/norton-big-four/ (gitignored).
# Image credits: Andy Tiernan Classics photographs via Sheldon's EMU, research
# reference only.
# Stage B note: +0.3955 pak units applied after preparation so the motorcycle,
# not the combined bounding box, sits at the tile centre.
#
# Rider and sidecar passenger added 2026-11-20 in Blender 4.5. One seated rider and
# one adult in the Oxford chair, in khaki period clothing and a black helmet. The
# passenger's crown sits about 0.24 m above the chair's front panel. Stylised
# low-poly reconstruction fitted to the master. Object names, dates and colours
# unchanged.

obj=citycar
name=NortonBigFourCombo(KhakiGreen)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=40
intro_year=1940
intro_month=1
retire_year=1948
retire_month=6
speed=80
class=0,1
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=190

Image[E]=./images/norton-big-four-combo-khaki_E.0.0,-33,14
